WhisperX is an enhanced version of Whisper (by OpenAI) with word-level timestamps, batching, and speaker diarization.
It is open-source under BSD-2-Clause.

Local build: whisperX 3.4.2 + local patches (see "Local patches" below). Upstream is at v3.8.6.
Last verified: 2026-09-27.

Prerequisites:
- ffmpeg
- Python 3.12. NOTE: the venv at ~/repos/whisperX/.venv is 3.12, not 3.14. Upstream added 3.13
  support and dropped 3.9; 3.14 is not a supported target. Don't "upgrade" this.
- cloned repo: https://github.com/m-bain/whisperX. This way you can update the code and use it.
  If you install it like the README suggests you can't update the code.
- HuggingFace account with access accepted for the diarization pipeline you actually use:
-- speaker-diarization-3.1   (https://huggingface.co/pyannote/speaker-diarization-3.1)
-- segmentation-3.0          (https://huggingface.co/pyannote/segmentation-3.0)
-- speaker-diarization-community-1 (https://huggingface.co/pyannote/speaker-diarization-community-1)
   ^ only usable once pyannote.audio 4.x is installed. See "The pyannote 4 question".

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEPS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. If the file to be processed isn't an acceptable file type, convert it to .wav:
   `ffmpeg -i <inputFile> -ar 16000 -ac 1 -c:a pcm_s16le <outputFile>`
   Check before re-converting: `ffprobe -v error -show_entries stream=codec_name,sample_rate,channels -of default=noprint_wrappers=1 <file>`
   You want pcm_s16le / 16000 / 1. If it already says that, skip this step.

2. Log in to HuggingFace so the python script can see the token.
   One option: `huggingface-cli login` and then paste the token.

3. From the project root (~/repos/whisperX):

   `uv run python -m whisperx <inputFile> --model large-v3 --language en --diarize --diarize_model pyannote/speaker-diarization-3.1 --min_speakers 5 --max_speakers 7 --device mps --compute_type float32 --batch_size 4 --beam_size 10 --initial_prompt "<comma-separated prompts>" --print_progress True --output_dir outputs`

   Everything that is NOT in that command, and used to be, was a default being restated.
   Verified against `uv run python -m whisperx --help` on 2026-09-27:
     --patience 1                            default is 1.0
     --length_penalty 1.0                    default is 1.0
     --temperature 0                         default is 0
     --temperature_increment_on_fallback 0.2  default is 0.2
     --compression_ratio_threshold 2.4        default is 2.4
     --logprob_threshold -1.0                 default is -1.0
     --vad_method pyannote                    default is pyannote
     --chunk_size 30                          default is 30
   Dropping them changes nothing and makes the flags that DO matter visible:
   --model, --language, --beam_size 10 (default 5), --batch_size 4 (default 8),
   --compute_type float32 (default float16, which fails on CPU).

3.1 'mps' only works because of a local patch. It maps --device mps to:
    ASR on cpu (CTranslate2 has no MPS backend) and diarization on mps.
    The script prints a line saying so. It is significantly faster than all-cpu.

3.2 --min_speakers / --max_speakers: the local patch treats min == max as an EXACT
    speaker count (it passes num_speakers=N instead of a range).
    Use equal values ONLY when you are confident every speaker talks a fair amount.
    If one person barely speaks, forcing the exact count makes pyannote manufacture a
    split: it will cut one talkative person into two labels to reach the number you
    demanded. This has happened twice and both are documented in the codex
    (2025_09_06_tarkusVell: Travis landed on both SPEAKER_04 and SPEAKER_00 because
    Jake barely spoke; 2024_01_05_dyingGrove: SPEAKER_04 carried two people).
    For a long session with unknown participation, give it a range and let it estimate.

3.3 DO NOT pass --output_format. You want the default (all), because you need two of them:
      <name>.spk.txt  the pipeline input (local patch, see below)
      <name>.json     the checkpoint that --diarize_from_json reads
    Restricting the format to spk throws away your ability to retry diarization.

3.4 These three were in the old command and do nothing for you:
      --highlight_words True --max_line_width 80 --max_line_count 1
    They only affect srt/vtt rendering and line breaking. WriteSPK ignores writer
    options entirely, so they have no effect on the .spk.txt you actually consume.

3.5 --no_speech_threshold was set to 0.3 (default 0.6). Lowering it makes Whisper MORE
    willing to discard a segment as silence, though only when decoding has also failed
    the logprob threshold. If quiet table talk ever goes missing, this is the knob to
    put back to 0.6 first. Left out of the command above.

4. RETRYING DIARIZATION WITHOUT REDOING ASR (local patch, and the most useful thing here)

   Before diarization the script writes the transcript out as a checkpoint. If the
   speaker labels come out wrong, do NOT re-run from scratch. Re-run diarization only:

   `uv run python -m whisperx <inputFile> --diarize_from_json outputs/<name>.json --diarize_model pyannote/speaker-diarization-3.1 --min_speakers 5 --max_speakers 7 --device mps --print_progress True --output_dir outputs`

   --diarize_from_json implies --diarize. It strips any speaker labels already in the
   checkpoint first, so stale labels from a previous run can't silently mix with new
   ones under the same names. Only one audio file at a time.
   On a nine-hour recording this turns a failed diarization from an overnight redo into
   a short retry, and it lets you sweep min/max speaker values cheaply.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
LOCAL PATCHES (uncommitted, five files, ~260 insertions)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Snapshot: local-m1-changes.patch. `git status` in ~/repos/whisperX shows:
  whisperx/__main__.py  whisperx/diarize.py  whisperx/transcribe.py
  whisperx/utils.py     whisperx/vads/pyannote.py

What they add, none of which is upstream at 3.4.2:
  1. WriteSPK / --output_format spk. Emits "[HH:MM:SS - HH:MM:SS] SPEAKER_XX: text",
     falling back to the majority word-level speaker, then to UNK. THIS IS LOAD-BEARING:
     every *.raw.spk.txt in the codex is this format and relabel_speakers.py reads it.
  2. --device mps, split as described in 3.1.
  3. --diarize_from_json plus the pre-diarization checkpoint write.
  4. min == max treated as an exact speaker count.
  5. pyannote 4.x compatibility: use_auth_token -> token, and handling for the 4.x
     DiarizeOutput object.
  6. Per-stage pyannote ProgressHook bars, and a progress counter in assign_word_speakers.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE PYANNOTE 4 QUESTION (why the Sep 22 upgrade did not stick)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Installed right now: pyannote.audio 3.3.2. torch 2.5.1. mps available.

pyannote/speaker-diarization-community-1 CANNOT run on that. Its own config.yaml says:
    dependencies:
      pyannote.audio: 4.0.0
    pipeline params: clustering: VBxClustering, plda: $model/plda
and the installed 3.3.2 exposes only AgglomerativeClustering and OracleClustering.
There is no VBxClustering and no plda in 3.x. The model is already downloaded and
gate-accepted in ~/.cache/huggingface/hub, so the only thing missing is the library.

WHY IT REVERTED: `uv run` re-syncs the venv to uv.lock on every invocation. uv.lock
pins pyannote-audio 3.3.2 (pyproject only asks for >=3.3.2). A pip upgrade inside the
venv gets wiped the next time you type `uv run`. That is what venv-freeze-before-pyannote4.txt
is a record of. To make it stick, change the dependency and re-lock, e.g.
  uv add "pyannote.audio>=4.0"
then confirm with
  uv run python -c "import importlib.metadata as m; print(m.version('pyannote.audio'))"

WHY IT IS WORTH DOING: community-1 uses VBx clustering instead of agglomerative.
VBx does not need a fixed cluster count and handles speakers with little speech far
better, which is the exact failure mode in 3.2 above. Separately, the local patch
prefers pyannote 4's exclusive_speaker_diarization, which contains no overlapping
speech turns. Overlapping turns are why a question and its answer keep landing on one
speaker label in these transcripts.

